# Project Setup
This codebase is outdated (7 years old) so there will be lots of issues if you are trying to install it on your local machine as a normal way due to oudated libraries.

A better approach is to use Docker for both dev and production. This project has been dockerized.

### Requirements

+ Docker: https://www.docker.com/

+ Docker compose: For those using Docker Desktop for Mac and Windows, it already included. For Linux users, please install Docker Engine and read further here: https://docs.docker.com/compose/install/

### Local Dev Setup

`docker-compose build`

### Start server

Comment below snippet in `docker-compose.yml`

```
  web:
    # environment:
      # RAILS_ENV: "${RAILS_ENV}"
```

`docker-compose up web`

### Create an empty database

`docker-compose run web bundle exec rake db:create`

### Import data

`docker-compose exec -T db mysql -u root -p --max_allowed_packet=512M connectedcity_dev < ../dump_db_file.sql`

Access rails c

```
City.unscoped.update_all is_active: true
```

## Issue with Solr configuration

The data must be indexed before you can start searching in the app. The following command can be used for 'reindexing', but it will cause errors

`docker-compose run web bundle exec sunspot:solr:reindex`

You might get the response from Solr with the exception: `undefined field type`. This is because Solr will try to clean up the original indexes first but because there is nothing there, it does not know how to clean up the indexes.

To work around this, go to Rails console

`docker-compose exec web rails console`

Then run the following Ruby code:

```
Rails.application.eager_load!
sunspot_models = Sunspot.searchable
sunspot_models.each do |model|
  model.solr_index()
end
```

You might get the following errors while the data is being indexed

`cannot set an index-time boost, unindexed or norms are omitted for field title_text`

To fix this, we need to modify the Solr schema to make `title_text` field include the attribute `omitNorms=false` on Solr. We can update this by running the following `curl` command

```
curl -X POST -H 'Content-type:application/json' --data-binary '{
  "replace-field":{
     "name":"title_text",
     "type":"strings",
     "omitNorms":false }
}' http://localhost:8983/solr/development/schema
```

In the above, your solr is running at `localhost:8983` and `development` is the name of your core

After running the above command successfully, go back to Rails console and retry. If you get the same error on other fields, repeat the above `curl` command but replace the `name` with the correct field name having the issue.

# Deployment

Follow the guideline here: https://devcenter.heroku.com/articles/container-registry-and-runtime

```
docker-compose build
docker-compose up web
docker-compose run web bundle exec rake assets:precompile RAILS_ENV=production

heroku container:login
heroku container:push web
heroku container:release web

heroku run bash
RAILS_ENV=production bundle exec rake db:migrate
```

 Note that all changes on your local will be deployed to server, not only committed code.